# PR Description

Generate a short, easy-to-read PR description based on the changes in the current branch. Keep it simple — no over-engineering, no walls of text.

## Workflow

1. **Find the base branch.** Try `main`, fall back to `master`:
   ```bash
   git rev-parse --verify origin/main >/dev/null 2>&1 && BASE=origin/main || BASE=origin/master
   ```
   If the user names a different base branch, use that instead.

2. **Collect the changes:**
   ```bash
   git log --oneline $BASE..HEAD
   git diff --stat $BASE..HEAD
   ```
   Only read the full diff (`git diff $BASE..HEAD`) for files where the commit messages and stats aren't enough to understand what changed. Don't dump the whole diff into context for large changes — sample the important files.

3. **Write the description** using the template below.

## Output template

```markdown
## Hvad

1-3 sentences: what this PR does, in plain language. Lead with the user/business effect, not the implementation.

## Hvorfor

1-2 sentences: the motivation (bug, feature request, tech debt, etc.). Skip this section if it's obvious from "Hvad".

## Ændringer

- 3-7 bullets max, grouped by area — not one bullet per commit or per file
- Mention breaking changes, migrations, or config changes explicitly if any

## Test

One line: how it was verified (unit tests, manually tested, not tested yet).
```

## Rules

- **Match the user's language.** If commits/conversation are in Danish, write the description in Danish; otherwise English. Section headers follow the same language (What / Why / Changes / Test).
- **Short beats complete.** The reviewer should understand the PR in under 30 seconds. Cut anything that doesn't help them.
- Never invent test coverage or motivation you can't see in the changes — write "Manually verified" only if the user said so, otherwise ask or write "TODO".
- Suggest a PR title on the first line: max ~70 chars, imperative mood (e.g. "Add retry logic to payment client"). Use conventional-commit prefix (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`) only if the repo's commit history already uses that style.
- Output the description in a fenced markdown block so it's easy to copy directly into GitHub/GitLab/Azure DevOps.
- If there are no commits ahead of the base branch, say so instead of inventing content.
